														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2173178">
									

<p>The thing that will make me downvote the article is the constant repetition of words.</p>
<p>You used the word &quot;location&quot; to a total of 26 times and the word &quot;book&quot; to a total of 16. This really damaged the flow of the article. As an example, on the second paragraph of the description, you wrote three lines and used the word &quot;location&quot; four times.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2260 has the ability to put subjects in a temporary coma and cause them to visualize the <strong>location</strong> specified in the writing. While they are not actually visiting the <strong>location</strong>, the details of the <strong>location</strong> are the exact same as the real <strong>location</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It reached a point where i had to stop reading a bit to try and visualize which &quot;location&quot; you were talking about. Synonymy should be your best friend when writing technical.</p>
<p>Other than that, it's mostly tone issues. I will highlight some.</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>&quot;and submit the paperwork to Dr. Matthews, who will send the paperwork over to O5-█.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Prolonged exposure to SCP-2260 has been shown to be <strong>very dangerous</strong>.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;which is far heavier than a normal composition book&quot;</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>So, i will -1 until you make a revision, upon which time i might reconsider my vote and give my opinions on the overall ellaboration of the article and the idea. Good luck.</p>

