		<div class="post" id="post-2173689">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2173689)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2173689">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/argenti-aertheri" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1409105); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1409105&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606142671" alt="Argenti Aertheri" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1409105)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/argenti-aertheri" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1409105); return false;" >Argenti Aertheri</a></span> <span class="odate time_1417894361 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Dec 2014 19:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2173689">
									

<p>I like the concept, but the grammar and style need help. So here goes!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;This&nbsp;is&nbsp;going&nbsp;to&nbsp;be&nbsp;long</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Make&nbsp;it&nbsp;go&nbsp;away!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Any personnel wishing to use SCP-2260 for any purpose must fill out the mandatory SCP-2260 paperwork detailing location of choice, time wishing to be spent there, and activities performed at said location and submit the paperwork to Dr. Matthews, who will transfer said paperwork over to O5-█.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Long sentence is long. Maybe say they must fill out the paperwork and transfer it, and then, in a new sentence, say what the paperwork entails? Also, just say no to O5 references, give the site or project director the authority to okay the scip's use.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The pages are wide ruled, contains 200 pages, appears to be recently purchased, and all other details of SCP-2260's appearance remain the same of any other composition book.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is all garbled, try something more like: &quot;SCP-2260 looks as though recently purchased, contains 200 wide ruled pages, and appears the same as a non-anomalous composition book.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2260 has the ability to put subjects in a temporary coma and cause them to visualize the location specified in the writing. While they are not actually visiting the real place, the details of the location are the exact same as the actuality. On top of this, anything that happens to them while at the site will affect them in real life.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;On top of this&quot; is really not a phrase you'd see in technical writing, stick to the basic &quot;also&quot;, &quot;additionally&quot;, &quot;further&quot; and such. I don't think I'd make that its own sentence though. I'd go more like:</p>
<p>&quot;Description: SCP-2260 is a green composition book with no markings or names on the front of the book. On the first page of the book, the sentence &quot;I went to spend the rest of my life in Hawaii&quot; is written in pencil. SCP-2260 looks as though recently purchased, contains 200 wide ruled pages, and appears the same as a non-anomalous composition book; however, it weighs 4.5&#160;kg, or approximately 10 lbs.</p>
<p>Use of SCP-2260 puts subjects in a temporary coma and causes them to visit a perfect replica of the location specified in the writing. Though the subject is not actually visiting the specified place, anything that happens to them while using SCP-2260 will affect them in real life.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It must be written in the past tense, and specify a place the writer wants to go to (ex. I went to Japan), and what length of time the writer wants to stay at said position (ex. I stayed for 2 days).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Breaking that up with the two examples, combined with the grammar, makes it read oddly. I'd go for &quot;It must be written in the past tense, and specify where the subject wishes to go, and for how long they wish to stay (e.g. 'I went to Japan and stayed 2 days.')&quot;. (Well there's some punctuation salad for ya!)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>…and the entry into the book will fade away.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's either an entry in the book, or an entry written into the book, but I'd use SCP-2260 instead of &quot;the book&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects sent to sites existing in the past have been proven to be 100% accurate, and tests done with near future sites have been proven to be exact as well.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That reads weird, but I don't know what to do with it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects that use SCP-2260 once a week or more have been prone to hallucinogenic episodes while awake, visiting places previously entered into SCP-2260.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Places they entered into the scip, or places that were previously entered by anyone?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject woke up and stated that someone will interrupt the test. 8 minutes later Dr. Matthews stepped in to see how the test was doing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Never start a sentence with a number that isn't written out, that should be &quot;Eight minutes…&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Testing has been done on his vitals while in the comatose state. SCP-2260 keeps all brain activity normal, and appears to regulate heart beat and other vital organs. It also regenerates cell structure 10 times quicker than that of a normal human. James has been put in the Cryogenics Area in Sector C for further testing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This probably shouldn't be four short sentences, something like this would probably work better:</p>
<p>&quot;Testing has been done on his vitals while in the comatose state, SCP-2260 appears to keep all brain activity normal and to regulate heart beat and other vital organs. It also regenerates cell structure 10 times quicker than that of a normal human, James has been put in the Cryogenics Area in Sector C for further testing.&quot;</p>
<p>I'd also call him SCP-2260-1 or something that isn't his given name.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Addendum 2260-C:<br />
D-████ made a personal request to re-use SCP-2260 after using it previously to go to [DATA EXPUNGED]. They were accepted the request, and another test was performed. D-████ made another request to use SCP-2260 only 2 days following the previous experiment, and the request was denied due to the short amount of time between testing. D-████ was told they could use it again in a month. After the month passed, another test was performed on D-████, who was very eager to comply with all commands issued. Upon waking from unconsciousness, D-████ immediately requested another use of SCP-2260. Once D-████ was denied, they went into a fit of rage and attacked themselves, security, and research personnel alike. They were apprehended, were told they could never use SCP-2260 again, and were sent into psychological counselling. A few weeks later, D-████ was found dead with a tongue chewed off, bloody fingernails, and scratches on the wall stating &quot;Without that book, I <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">have</span> am nothing&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Your technical writing just fell apart at the seams.</p>
<p>&quot;They were accepted the request&quot;?</p>
<p>Maybe go for: &quot;D-████ made another request to use SCP-2260 only 2 days following the previous experiment. This request was denied due to the short amount of time since the previous test, D-████ was, however, told they could use it again in a month.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;After the month passed&quot; isn't very clinical, &quot;when allowed to use SCP-2260 again&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon waking from unconsciousness, D-████ immediately requested another use of SCP-2260. Once D-████ was denied, they went into a fit of rage and attacked themselves, security, and research personnel alike. They were apprehended, were told they could never use SCP-2260 again, and were sent into psychological counselling.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is storytelling, not technical writing. I'd change it to:</p>
<p>&quot;Upon waking D-████ immediately requested another use of SCP-2260, the request was denied. The subject then exhibited extreme rage, attacking themselves, security, and research personnel. D-████ was apprehended and remanded to psychological counseling. Subject is barred from further contact with SCP-2260.&quot;</p>
<p>I feel like I'm being too critical, so I'm gonna note that I wouldn't be bothering to type this all up if I didn't like the concept and want to see it succeed &#8212; I'd just downvote and move on with my life. And I do really like the idea that a seemingly silly little thing to take a vacation with can cause people to kill themselves. But…</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;A few weeks later, D-████ was found dead with a tongue chewed off, bloody fingernails, and scratches on the wall stating &quot;Without that book, I <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">have</span> am nothing&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm assuming the D-class chewed off his own tongue, but as it stands, somebody else could've chewed off their tongue, or made the scratches on the wall and yeah, this time I would use pronouns &#8212; &quot;was found with his/her tongue chewed off&quot;.</p>
<p>Also, after that, a note about &quot;researchers are advised to exercise caution when using the same subject for repeat experiments&quot;. Maybe &quot;as it appears that SCP-2260 causes addiction in some subjects, researches are advised…&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While D-████ was an extreme case, other D-class subjects have made requests to re-use SCP-2260, despite how dangerous the previous test may have been.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;…despite risk of bodily harm&quot; &#8212; the Foundation wouldn't tell them about what happened to previous subjects (it's <em>really bad</em> experimental design to do that, ever) and the reader already knows it's dangerous, so just go with the vague threat. That leaves open the possibility that the risk is from security, not the scip.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One D-class subject repeatedly said &quot;Give me the book&quot; and refused to eat, sleep, or even move. Upon another test with SCP-2260, they resumed a normal physique until 2 days had passed, in which they would not do anything until a test was performed with SCP-2260. They are currently still in psychological counselling.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I…no, just no. Ditch &quot;upon&quot;, &quot;resumed a normal physique&quot; makes no sense in context, &quot;currently still&quot; is repetitive, and the whole thing is silly since they'd never give him his demanded test anyways. That's not an experiment, that's giving a D-class what they demand for the sake of it. I'd just drop the whole paragraph and work the fact the won't eat or sleep into the last one.</p>
<p>Addendum 2260-D has &quot;there was&quot; which should be &quot;there were&quot;.</p>
<p>I'm just gonna strike out my issues here, since they're minor, and I'm sure you're as bored of this as I am!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Other Experiments:</p>
<p>Test on 3/4/██: Entry was written on a loose-leaf paper and <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">stuffed</span> <em>inserted</em> into the book. No effect took place.</p>
<p>Test on 5/██/██: Entry was written in the book, and the page was immediately torn out. <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Subject slipped into unconsciousness and went to specified location. Entry faded when subject awakened.</span> <em>No change to SCP-2260's performance was noted.</em></p>
<p>Test on 6/14/1█: A page was torn out <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">of book,</span> and the entry was written on the <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">torn out</span> page. No effect took place.</p>
<p>Test on 6/2█/1█: The entry was written, and immediately <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">whited out</span> <em>blacked out</em>. <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Words in</span> <em>the entry burned through the <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">white out</span> //ink</em>, and were visible after 9 seconds. [White out is a brand name and as such wouldn't be used this way in technical writing. Anyways, we known he Foundtion loves their black boxes!]</p>
<p>Test on 7/██/██: The entry was written, and the page was carefully burned <em>so</em> as to not burn the book. Once <em>the</em> page was burned away, the entry appeared on the next page.</p>
<p>Test on 8/7/1█: <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">The</span> <em>An</em> entry was written without an event specified, and once subject <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">went into</span> <em>became</em> unconscious<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">ness</span>, a secondary subject wrote in an event. Secondary subject also fell into unconsciousness, and when both <em>simultaneously</em> awoke <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">(at the same time), both</span> <em>they</em> claimed to have seen <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">the</span> <em>each</em> other at the location.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A few more things: your blacking out of dates is inconsistent; I'd number the tests, and make them experiments instead of tests; there's no real need to black out D-class numbers, if anything, it makes it harder to be sure it's the same D-class being referenced.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That was really long, and I'm sorry if it came off too critical. I do really like this idea, and hope it can be cleaned up and saved.</p>

							</div>

								
					
