<p>No problem! I was worried that I'd gotten way too verbose, glad to see my endless critique was useful :)</p>
<p>Edit: looks like I missed a couple &#8212;</p>
<p>&quot;Light headedness and temporary amnesia has also been recorded in subjects.&quot; &#8212; should be have, not has.</p>
<p>And &quot;after a few weeks&quot; should probably be a number, or black boxed, not &quot;a few&quot; (basically, when doing technical writing, nothing is ever estimated unless it cannot be known, eg that the subject that died of a heart attack died in the location he was visiting).</p>
<p>Also, and this is me being nitpicky, APA standards say to either use &quot;they&quot; or &quot;the participant&quot; (psych research doesn't call them subjects anymore, it's dehumanizing…which is exactly why the Foundation uses it), or to alternate between masculine and feminine pronouns. So make some of your subjects female perhaps?</p>
<p>*is glad to be putting all that undergrad writing to use*</p>
<p>Edit 2: lightheadedness is one word.</p>
<p>&quot;…and began to start breathing faster at an expodential rate.&quot; &#8212; &quot;began to start is redundant and &quot;expodential&quot; is probably not what you want. More like: &quot;…and began to breath increasingly quickly&quot; or &quot;…and breathing became increasing fast&quot;.</p>
<p>And check the tests again, looks like a placeholder number got left in and that you've got a &quot;book&quot; missing its &quot;the&quot; (addendum A and E respectively).</p>
<p>Another edit: &quot;and <em>events seen by</em> subjects sent into sites existing in the future have happened precisely as shown.&quot;</p>

